{
  "id": "Compound::DB00860 - Disease::DOID:7147",
  "verbalization": "Prednisolone, a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist, is implicated in the treatment of Ankylosing Spondylitis through its inclusion in the class of therapeutic glucocorticoids. This class of drugs is known to modulate inflammatory processes by inhibiting the activity of pro-inflammatory mediators, such as cytokines and enzymes, which are central to the pathogenesis of Ankylosing Spondylitis. The evidence from the visible paths suggests that Prednisolone, through its relationship with Betamethasone and Triamcinolone, exerts a therapeutic effect on the disease, likely by targeting the corticosteroid receptor pathway. The shared ancestry of Prednisolone with these compounds, along with their common therapeutic classification, supports a mechanistic link that is consistent with the known pharmacological actions of glucocorticoids. The integration of these paths highlights a robust, biologically plausible mechanism by which Prednisolone may repurpose to treat Ankylosing Spondylitis, emphasizing the role of corticosteroid receptor activation in modulating inflammation.",
